---
layout: layouts/page.njk
permalink: /
bodyClass: page page--home
---
<div class="home" data-pagefind-body>
  {#
    The site's name is the page's heading, and what it is for is the line under
    it. The name was an eyebrow over a tagline set as the H1, which made the
    first heading of the site a sentence about the site rather than the site —
    and left two lines of description under it saying much the same thing twice.
  #}
  <header class="home__hero">
    <h1 class="home__title">{{ site.title }}</h1>
    <p class="home__lede">{{ site.tagline }}</p>

    {#
      Search, before any list of games.

      The home page is where somebody arrives without a game in mind, and the
      header's trigger is a small control in the corner among three others —
      fine as a tool while reading, too quiet to be the way in. So this is the
      same search, said at the size of the job: one field-shaped control across
      the column, above everything it searches.

      It is not a second search box. There is one search surface on this site and
      this opens it, which is why it is a button and not an input — an input here
      would have to grow its own results list, its own keyboard handling and its
      own idea of scope, and then be kept in step with the palette's. Typing on
      it still works: the first keystroke opens the panel and lands in the field.

      Hidden until the script that opens it runs, like the header's trigger.
    #}
    <button
      type="button"
      class="home-search"
      data-palette-open
      aria-haspopup="dialog"
      aria-label="Search all games"
      hidden>
      <svg class="home-search__icon" viewBox="0 0 20 20" width="20" height="20" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
        <circle cx="9" cy="9" r="5.5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8"/>
        <path d="M13.2 13.2 17 17" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" stroke-linecap="round"/>
      </svg>
      <span class="home-search__label">Search every game, section and term</span>
      <kbd class="home-search__key" aria-hidden="true" data-shortcut-key>Ctrl K</kbd>
    </button>

    {#
      What is new, under the field.

      One line, the most recent only, and nothing at all when nothing has
      happened lately — see `src/_data/updates.js`. A shelf sorted
      alphabetically cannot say what arrived this month, and a reader coming
      back has no other way to find out.

      The whole line is the link when there is somewhere to go, so the target
      is the size of the sentence rather than of a word inside it.
    #}
    {% if updates.current %}
      {% set news = updates.current %}
      {% if news.url %}
        <a class="home-update" href="{{ news.url }}">
          <span class="home-update__tag">{{ news.tag }}</span>
          {#
            The arrow rides at the end of the sentence rather than at the end
            of the row: on a phone the line wraps, and a flex sibling would sit
            out at the top right with nothing beside it.
          #}
          <span class="home-update__text">{{ news.text }}
            <span class="home-update__go" aria-hidden="true">→</span></span>
        </a>
      {% else %}
        <p class="home-update">
          <span class="home-update__tag">{{ news.tag }}</span>
          <span class="home-update__text">{{ news.text }}</span>
        </p>
      {% endif %}
    {% endif %}
  </header>

  {#
    Favorites, then recent. Both are shells the client fills, because only the
    browser knows what is in them — and both stay hidden until there is
    something to put in them, so a first-time reader meets the games and
    nothing else.

    Favorites sits above because it is the deliberate list: a reader who has
    starred a game has said it matters more than the fact that they opened
    something yesterday. Recent drops anything already starred, so the two rows
    never show the same game twice.
  #}
  <section class="home__section" aria-labelledby="favorites-title" data-favorite-games hidden>
    <h2 class="section-title" id="favorites-title">Favorite Games</h2>
    <ul class="game-grid game-grid--recent" data-favorite-list></ul>
  </section>

  {#
    Recently opened. Empty and hidden until the history says otherwise, which
    it can only do from the browser — so this stays a shell the client fills.
  #}
  <section class="home__section" aria-labelledby="recent-title" data-recent-games hidden>
    <h2 class="section-title" id="recent-title">Recent Games</h2>
    <ul class="game-grid game-grid--recent" data-recent-list></ul>
  </section>

  <section class="home__section" aria-labelledby="games-title">
    <h2 class="section-title" id="games-title">
      All Games
      <span class="section-title__count" data-shelf-count>{{ listed | length }}</span>
    </h2>

    {#
      Sorting and filtering the shelf.

      Shipped `hidden` and revealed by `shelf.js`, like every other control
      that cannot work without it — and everything it does is done by moving
      and hiding tiles that are already on the page, so a reader without a
      script still gets the whole shelf, alphabetically, which is the order
      the build writes.

      Two filters, both ranges, both about the table rather than about the
      game: who is here and how long we have got. Weight and tags were the
      obvious third and fourth and neither earns a control yet — every game on
      this shelf is heavy, and most tags are on one game each.
    #}
    <div class="shelf-tools" data-shelf-tools hidden>
      <div class="shelf-tools__row">
        <label class="shelf-field">
          <span class="shelf-field__label">Sort by</span>
          <select class="shelf-select" data-shelf-sort>
            <option value="title">Name</option>
            <option value="year">Release date</option>
          </select>
        </label>

        {#
          Direction is its own control rather than four entries in the list
          above: "A to Z / Z to A" and "newest / oldest first" are the same
          question asked twice, and a list that spells out every combination
          grows by multiplication every time a sort is added.
        #}
        <button type="button" class="shelf-direction" data-shelf-direction
                aria-pressed="false">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 16 16" width="14" height="14" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
            <path d="M8 13V3M4.5 6.5 8 3l3.5 3.5" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
                  stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/>
          </svg>
          <span data-shelf-direction-label>Ascending</span>
        </button>

        <button type="button" class="shelf-reset" data-shelf-reset hidden>Clear filters</button>
      </div>

      <div class="shelf-tools__row shelf-tools__row--filters">
        {% from "partials/range-slider.njk" import rangeSlider %}
        {{ rangeSlider("players", "Players", shelf.players, "player") }}
        {{ rangeSlider("time", "Playing time", shelf.time, "minute") }}
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="shelf-empty" data-shelf-empty hidden>
      No games match those filters.
    </p>

    <ul class="game-grid" data-shelf-list>
      {% for slug, item in listed %}
        {% set players = item.meta.players | numberRange %}
        {% set time = item.meta.time | numberRange %}
        {#
          Everything the shelf sorts and filters by, on the tile itself. The
          page is the index: there is no second copy of the catalogue to fetch
          or to keep in step.
        #}
        <li class="game-tile" data-game-slug="{{ slug }}"
            data-title="{{ item.title }}"
            data-year="{{ item.meta.year_published or '' }}"
            {% if players %}data-players-min="{{ players.min }}" data-players-max="{{ players.max }}"{% endif %}
            {% if time %}data-time-min="{{ time.min }}" data-time-max="{{ time.max }}"{% endif %}>
          {#
            The box art is shown as itself: contained in a fixed-ratio frame on
            a quiet ground, so covers of different proportions still line up
            across the grid without being cropped. Nothing is laid over the
            art — the title sits below it, where it can be set in the same type
            as the rest of the site.
          #}
          <a class="game-tile__link" href="{{ item.url }}">
            {% if item.meta.box_art %}
              <span class="game-tile__art">
                <img class="game-tile__image" src="{{ item.meta.box_art }}" alt="" sizes="320px">
              </span>
            {% endif %}
          </a>

          <div class="game-tile__body">
            <h3 class="game-tile__title"><a href="{{ item.url }}">{{ item.title }}</a></h3>
            <p class="game-tile__meta">
              {{ item.meta.designer }}{% if item.meta.year_published %} · {{ item.meta.year_published }}{% endif %}
            </p>
            <p class="game-tile__description">{{ item.meta.description }}</p>
            <ul class="chip-row">
              {% for type in item.contentTypes %}
                <li><a class="chip" href="{{ type.url }}">{{ type.label }}</a></li>
              {% endfor %}
              {% for expansion in item.expansions %}
                <li><a class="chip chip--muted" href="{{ expansion.contentTypes[0].url }}">{{ expansion.title }}</a></li>
              {% endfor %}
            </ul>
          </div>
        </li>
      {% endfor %}
    </ul>
  </section>
</div>
